meta {
  name: 40 - Labels Bare
  type: graphql
  seq: 40
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}
  body: graphql
  auth: inherit
}

body:graphql {
  query LabelsBare {
    issueLabels(first: 250, includeArchived: true, orderBy: updatedAt) {
      nodes {
        id
        createdAt
        updatedAt
        archivedAt
        name
        description
        color
        isGroup
        lastAppliedAt
        retiredAt
        team { id }
        parent { id }
        creator { id }
        inheritedFrom { id }
      }
      pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }
    }
  }
}

docs {
  Reference: https://linear.app/developers/graphql

  The canonical Labels selection set — verbatim what the connector will send.

  **This stream is the odd one out in the cluster, in one specific way:** `issueLabels`
  does NOT accept the `sort` argument (see `42 - Labels Sort Rejected`). It has only
  `orderBy`, which `04 - OrderBy Direction Probe` proved is DESCENDING-only. So while
  Issues / Projects / Initiatives sweep ascending and checkpoint per page, Labels must
  sweep newest-first and checkpoint ONCE at end-of-sweep, using the maximum `updatedAt`
  observed across the whole sweep. Everything else about the stream — the connection
  shape, `first`/`after` pagination, the `IssueLabelFilter.updatedAt` comparator,
  `includeArchived` — is identical to its siblings, so it stays in the cluster and shares
  the engine; only the checkpoint policy differs.

  End-of-sweep checkpointing is cheap here specifically because label populations are
  small — tens to low hundreds per workspace, versus potentially millions of issues. The
  page size is set to the 250 maximum for that reason: most workspaces will complete this
  stream in a single request.

  Static cost under the documented formula (10 scalars, 4 relation objects): per node =
  1 + 10 x 0.1 + 4 x 1.1 = ~6.4 points; at `first: 250` that is ~1,600 points.

  **Workspace-level vs team-level labels — both are returned by this one root field.**
  `03 - Introspect Entity Types` showed `IssueLabel.team` is NULLABLE, and that nullability
  is the discriminator:

  - `team == null`  -> a WORKSPACE-level label, available to every team.
  - `team != null`  -> a label scoped to exactly that one team.

  `issueLabels` returns BOTH populations mixed together in a single connection, with no
  filter applied by default — so no partition is hidden and no separate sweep is needed.
  Selecting `team { id }` is what makes the distinction recoverable downstream, which is
  why it is in the selection set rather than being dropped as an incidental relation. Two
  labels in different teams may share a `name`, so consumers must not treat `name` as a
  key — `id` is the only primary key.

  Also selected: `isGroup` and `parent { id }`, which together express Linear's label
  GROUPS (a group is a label with `isGroup: true`; its members carry `parent`). And
  `inheritedFrom { id }`, which links a team label back to the workspace label it was
  inherited from.

  **Limitation / observed finding** (confirmed 2026-07-30): returns 3 rows — `Bug`,
  `Feature`, `Improvement` — all with `archivedAt: null`, all sharing the bulk-seed
  `updatedAt` of `2026-07-30T21:35:03.808Z`, and `hasNextPage: false`. All three are
  Linear's stock defaults. This workspace has a single team, so the team-scoped-label case
  is present in the schema but has no live example.
}

example {
  name: 200 — full representative IssueLabel (3 workspace-level labels)

  request: {
    url: https://api.linear.app/graphql
    method: POST
    mode: graphql

    body:graphql: {
      query LabelsBare {
        issueLabels(first: 250, includeArchived: true, orderBy: updatedAt) {
          nodes {
            id
            createdAt
            updatedAt
            archivedAt
            name
            description
            color
            isGroup
            lastAppliedAt
            retiredAt
            team { id }
            parent { id }
            creator { id }
            inheritedFrom { id }
          }
          pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  response: {
    headers: {
      x-complexity: 1000
      x-ratelimit-requests-limit: 2500
      x-ratelimit-requests-remaining: 2481
      x-ratelimit-complexity-limit: 3000000
      x-ratelimit-complexity-remaining: 2999000
    }

    status: {
      code: 200
      text: OK
    }

    body: {
      type: json
      content: '''
        {
          "data": {
            "issueLabels": {
              "nodes": [
                {
                  "id": "d3ffe6ef-6b5a-4af7-8b25-707b9282065c",
                  "createdAt": "2026-07-30T21:35:03.808Z",
                  "updatedAt": "2026-07-30T21:35:03.808Z",
                  "archivedAt": null,
                  "name": "Bug",
                  "description": null,
                  "color": "#EB5757",
                  "isGroup": false,
                  "lastAppliedAt": null,
                  "retiredAt": null,
                  "team": null,
                  "parent": null,
                  "creator": null,
                  "inheritedFrom": null
                },
                {
                  "id": "57cc6a96-8c08-4c63-b749-dea0c5ceb65c",
                  "createdAt": "2026-07-30T21:35:03.808Z",
                  "updatedAt": "2026-07-30T21:35:03.808Z",
                  "archivedAt": null,
                  "name": "Feature",
                  "description": null,
                  "color": "#BB87FC",
                  "isGroup": false,
                  "lastAppliedAt": null,
                  "retiredAt": null,
                  "team": null,
                  "parent": null,
                  "creator": null,
                  "inheritedFrom": null
                },
                {
                  "id": "274d6628-3101-41c1-96bd-27241c8301b5",
                  "createdAt": "2026-07-30T21:35:03.808Z",
                  "updatedAt": "2026-07-30T21:35:03.808Z",
                  "archivedAt": null,
                  "name": "Improvement",
